    "overview": "Learners begin by predicting how well they can now explain the opening phenomenon (frogs near Kisumu laying thousands of eggs, hens laying shelled eggs, cows bearing one calf, and IVF babies in Kenyan news). They revisit the dissected small mammal (or annotated photographs) in a Predict-Observe-Explain review to link structure to function, then take part in a structured debate on joint reproductive health choices and the responsible use of IVF, surrogacy and sperm banks, using hormone flowcharts as evidence. A guest speaker (midwife, nurse or clinical officer from a local health centre, or a recorded interview) answers learner questions. Learners then review and close the Driving Question Board, compare their Lesson 1 model to a final synthesis model, and write a Final Explanation that answers the driving question: How do different animals ensure successful fertilisation and development of offspring, and how can science assist when human reproduction fails?",
